00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.290
Hello there!

00:00:02.290 --> 00:00:04.300
How do vision transformers work?

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:07.570
Ah, this is what I have been asking myself
lately too!

00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:12.440
We’re already tired of hearing news about
Vision Transformers beating state-of-the-art

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:15.389
here, and there, everywhere!

00:00:15.389 --> 00:00:19.220
– everywhere in computer vision means ImageNet.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:23.590
But we have also come to see vision transformers
with convolutional biases in them,

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:28.980
like the hierarchical Swin Transformer which
has a limited attention window and we started

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:32.140
to get some mixed feelings about what works
best.

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:36.480
Is it now convolutions or is it multi-head
self-attention?

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:40.750
In the last video, we got entirely confused
about who wins the fight between ConvNets

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:42.120
and Transformers:

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:48.250
We saw with ConvNeXt how a modernized ConvNet
can compete with transformers.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:54.640
So, it’s time to read and explain this paper
that brings more insight into what makes vision

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:58.460
transformers so special
(multi-head self-attention!).

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:03.820
And why they are maybe not as special as we
think or not for the reasons we originally

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:04.940
had in mind.

