00:02:18.909 --> 00:02:25.950
Great, let’s go back to today’s topic
of comparing multi-head self-attention with

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:26.950
convolutions.

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:31.599
We got mixed signals about Vision Transformers
during the last decades in Machine Learning…

00:02:31.599 --> 00:02:33.689
eh, whom are we kidding?

00:02:33.689 --> 00:02:40.900
ViT was published September 2020 which admittedly
feels like a decade ago, because of the insane

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:42.379
pace of the field.

00:02:42.379 --> 00:02:47.730
If you want a recap about Vision Transformers,
do check out our playlist about them.

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:53.809
Here we make a quick recap on Multi-Head Self-Attention
or short MSA and convolutions.

00:02:53.809 --> 00:02:59.340
The convolutional layer involves a kernel,
basically a small matrix containing some numbers

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:03.930
that slides over the input and processes every
region in the same manner:

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:08.980
It’s the same kernel values that get multiplied
with the different values of the input.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:15.620
So, the parameters are shared between image
locations, inducing translation equivariance

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:21.109
since if we move the input across the image
and we apply the same weights everywhere in

00:03:21.109 --> 00:03:26.260
the image, the output will remain the same
but translate accordingly.

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:31.699
Sharing parameters, so having the same parameters
independently of the image region, is also

00:03:31.699 --> 00:03:35.579
a big plus if one wants to save memory.

00:03:35.579 --> 00:03:41.430
But then there is Multi-Head Self-Attention
or MSA in short that laughs at the whole concept

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:47.120
of sharing parameters across the spatial dimension
and instead dynamically computes a weight

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:48.670
for each token.

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:52.909
We remember the sequence spans across the
spatial dimension.

00:03:52.909 --> 00:03:54.499
Let’s break this down:

00:03:54.499 --> 00:03:59.599
We remember that 16 by 16 image patches are
represented as vectors, or in other words,

00:03:59.599 --> 00:04:00.599
image tokens.

00:04:00.599 --> 00:04:05.450
To compute a new representation for this image
token here, first, attention scores are computed

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:10.900
between this token and every other token in
the sequence, including oneself.

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:17.380
Then, the representations of these image tokens
are aggregated by a weighted sum in which

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:21.470
the weight of each token is given by the attention
score.

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:26.280
So, in this example here, this token contributes
a lot for computing this new representation,

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:28.760
this other token only very little.

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:31.220
Of course, we do this for all tokens.

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:36.690
So before, we had convolutions that compute
a new representation for each pixel by taking

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:42.500
the sum between all the pixels in a region
weighted by the entries of the kernel.

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:45.430
And now, we have the self-attention layer.

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:50.140
The result of which is a weighted average
of all these image vectors that represent

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:55.070
patches (like with convolutions), but now
the weights of the sum are

00:04:55.070 --> 00:05:03.180
first: positive, and second dynamically computed
through this renowned formula where we remember

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:07.830
that the data itself is considered here in
the keys and queries.

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:12.770
In other words, these attention scores are
highly data dependent and not static like

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:15.100
the kernel values of the convolution.

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:20.150
Don’t be confused: We are talking about
inference here or about one iteration during

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:21.150
training.

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:26.200
Of course, kernel values update during training
so they can adapt to the data and the task

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:27.200
at hand.

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:33.120
When we say that Convs are static we mean
that at one iteration or at inference time,

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:39.210
they are fixed and do not depend on the data
sample or spatial region in the data, while

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:43.490
attention weights are computed for each data
vector individually.

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:48.150
Another difference between MSAs and Convs
is that for MSAs, the weighted aggregation

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:52.550
is done over the entire input,
so unlike convolutions in CNNs that are usually

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:58.610
small windows like 3x3 and encompass a small
spatial range in the image,

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:03.050
self-attention encompasses a much longer range:
the whole image.

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:07.000
And why did we say that MSA laughs at the
concept of saving memory?

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:14.280
Well, while self-attention computes one score
between each two image tokens, multi-head

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:15.280
self-attention says:

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:16.280
Eh, come on!

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:20.190
Why decide for only one set of scores when
I can have 2.

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:22.140
Or 6, or 12!

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:27.680
In this way, the network does not have to
rely on one combination of attention scores,

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:31.120
but on many of them that have the chance to
specialize.

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:34.850
This idea follows the motto that ensembling
is in general beneficial.

00:06:34.850 --> 00:06:41.260
In a similar manner, convolutional networks
do not work with only one kernel per layer,

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:45.910
but with more of them where the weights can
be adapted during training to specialize for

00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:47.580
specific features.

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:53.350
Great, this was the short recap on MSAs versus
Convs, and we basically only explained the

00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:56.160
background of this little paragraph in the
paper.

00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:01.590
So, assuming we already know the key components
of Vision Transformers, during the last one

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:06.920
and a half year in which we had time to study
them, we still got quite the mixed signals

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:09.950
about what is now exactly the difference between
Convs and MSAs.

00:07:09.950 --> 00:07:14.550
And this is what I love about this paper:
it puts all of this together to get a more

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:19.160
coherent picture about what exactly makes
vision transformers great.

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:23.460
The introduction of the paper reminds us of
what we have been saying previously in our

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:24.560
videos too.

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:29.810
We think that the success of transformers
in general and MSA in particular, is due to

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:31.960
two main reasons:

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:35.300
First, the weak inductive bias of MSA.

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:39.670
While convolutions have this parameter-sharing
property throughout the spatial dimension,

00:07:39.670 --> 00:07:40.970
MSA doesn’t.

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:47.060
So, each image region can be handled by its
own specific attention weights.

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:52.180
Even more, these attention weights are data-specific,
as we discussed earlier.

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:57.419
So there are more degrees of freedom when
modelling with MSA than with convolutions

00:07:57.419 --> 00:08:02.601
and the network has the chance to discover
translation equivariance on its own and even

00:08:02.601 --> 00:08:06.100
cooler properties, if they are useful for
minimizing the loss!

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:10.810
And because there is less guidance from the
start for the network, Vision Transformers

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:13.780
are notorious to overfit on small datasets.

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:17.740
It shall remain to be seen in this video whether
this is true.

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:23.020
But we know that techniques involving heavy
data augmentation are needed for small data

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:29.810
regimes as we have seen with DeiT making ViT
work on smaller datasets.

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:35.529
The second reason is the extremely large context
window of vanilla MSA that computes attention

00:08:35.529 --> 00:08:39.250
scores for each token based on every single
other token.

00:08:39.250 --> 00:08:42.659
Attending to a wider context should lead to
better performance, right?

00:08:42.659 --> 00:08:43.659
Right?

00:08:43.659 --> 00:08:48.040
Let’s follow this paper’s related work
to see why this is not really the case.

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:52.620
Modelling long-range dependencies does not
necessarily result in higher performance.

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:56.041
There are examples in the literature showing
how a

00:08:56.041 --> 00:09:01.680
limited context window of MSA is beneficial
on small and large datasets alike, like we

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:05.120
have seen with the Swin Transformer in one
of our previous videos.

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:10.759
And then, there’s these facts we should
make sense of: Adding MSA to CNNs… helps,

00:09:10.759 --> 00:09:14.600
especially when MSA is added closer to the
last layer.

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:21.040
And then, we cite “ViTs are robust against
data corruption and image occlusions” and

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:26.860
especially against adversarial attacks where
the attack consists of introducing high-frequency

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:27.860
noise.

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:33.709
So, what is it with MSA and the insensitivity
to high frequencies, now?

00:09:33.709 --> 00:09:38.889
Ok, hold your questions because this paper
is going to address them by

00:09:38.889 --> 00:09:44.860
hypothesising that MSA is “a trainable spatial
smoothing of feature maps”.

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:50.910
And this makes sense when looking at the formula
of self-attention: We take the image vectors,

00:09:50.910 --> 00:09:55.720
or features across the spatial dimension,
and average them with importance-weights that

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:56.910
are all positive.

00:09:56.910 --> 00:10:03.350
So, the authors go ahead and ask three questions:
“What properties of MSAs do we need to better

00:10:03.350 --> 00:10:04.959
optimize NNs?”,

00:10:04.959 --> 00:10:08.360
“Do MSAs act like Convs?

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:10.959
If not, how are they different?”

00:10:10.959 --> 00:10:15.070
and third, “How can we harmonize MSAs with
Convs?”.

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:20.410
Following this last question, they propose
an architecture combining MSA and Convolutions

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:23.800
based on the lessons learned from answering
the first two questions.

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:28.540
Before going further into this, Ms. Coffee
Bean wants me to make a small comment on how

00:10:28.540 --> 00:10:31.319
the authors arrived at their conclusions.

00:10:31.319 --> 00:10:36.410
The authors answered these questions by conducting
their experiments on…

00:10:36.410 --> 00:10:37.690
CIFAR only.

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:43.990
I mean, it is great that they compare different
models like the ResNet-50 (which is a ConvNet)

00:10:43.990 --> 00:10:47.029
and transformers like ViT, PiT, and Swin.

00:10:47.029 --> 00:10:51.310
It’s problematic again that they use the
tiny versions of these models.

00:10:51.310 --> 00:10:55.620
But we have two questions: What about datasets
other than CIFAR?

00:10:55.620 --> 00:11:00.660
And how do the lessons of this paper generalize
for tasks other than image classification,

00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:04.389
so for something like object detection or
image segmentation?

00:11:04.389 --> 00:11:06.619
Keep this in mind throughout this video.

00:11:06.619 --> 00:11:11.690
Let’s follow the paper and answer the first
question: “What properties of MSAs do we

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:13.319
need to improve optimization?”.

00:11:13.319 --> 00:11:19.189
Well, the first lesson here is unsurprising:
It seems like MSA is acting like a more generalized

00:11:19.189 --> 00:11:20.410
spatial smoothing.

00:11:20.410 --> 00:11:23.330
Generalized, because it is trainable and data-dependent.

00:11:23.330 --> 00:11:26.249
And this is a good inductive bias for image
data.

00:11:26.249 --> 00:11:30.800
Only think about the data-specificity: it’s
a nice thing to have, something we do not

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:36.170
find in a data-independent aggregation layer
like we have in the MLP-Mixer.

00:11:36.170 --> 00:11:40.639
Nothing against the MLP-Mixer, it’s really
useful if we dislike the compute and memory

00:11:40.639 --> 00:11:44.779
footprint of MSA and are ready for a compromise
in accuracy.

00:11:44.779 --> 00:11:50.459
The surprising bit is that due to the weaker
inductive bias of MSA, the training of MSA-based

00:11:50.459 --> 00:11:52.869
neural networks is more difficult:

00:11:52.869 --> 00:11:57.130
They do not even overfit small training datasets!

00:11:57.130 --> 00:12:04.240
We see here how the two curves for training
and test of ViT on CIFAR-100 are very close

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:10.529
to each other and are not exhibiting the gap
specific to overfitting, not even in the small

00:12:10.529 --> 00:12:11.529
data regimes.

00:12:11.529 --> 00:12:16.339
Of course, this is also helped by the strong
DeiT like data augmentation the authors apply

00:12:16.339 --> 00:12:17.459
to ViT.

00:12:17.459 --> 00:12:22.420
Also interesting, but not surprising after
we have learned about Swin Transformers and

00:12:22.420 --> 00:12:29.100
ConvNeXts, is that not the huge context window
of MSA is the recipe to success, but a reduction

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:34.029
of the degrees of freedom by limiting MSA
to very small windows.

00:12:34.029 --> 00:12:40.300
We see in this picture how the lowest test
error is achieved with a 3x3 window size for

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:41.300
MSA.

00:12:41.300 --> 00:12:45.620
But Ms. Coffee Bean must comment again on
this because the authors conduct experiments

00:12:45.620 --> 00:12:51.649
on CIFAR-100 where we would assume that long
dependencies are not key for good performance.

00:12:51.649 --> 00:12:58.860
We expect this lesson to vary on more complex
datasets and on tasks other than image recognition.

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:02.290
Image recognition is too holistic for this,
I guess.

00:13:02.290 --> 00:13:06.480
Describing the whole image with one label
is not very challenging and we would like

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:11.570
to see results on other tasks like action
recognition where it is important to model

00:13:11.570 --> 00:13:15.640
how two different corners of the image interact
with one another.

00:13:15.640 --> 00:13:22.139
But a cool thing to find out is that MSA comes
with both advantages and disadvantages.

00:13:22.139 --> 00:13:26.480
On the bright side, MSAs flatten the loss
landscapes.

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:32.160
A flat landscape means better performance
and generalization because you know, landing

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:36.649
here or landing there on the loss landscape
does not change much.

00:13:36.649 --> 00:13:41.309
In a less flat landscape, it means a lot if
I land here or over there.

00:13:41.309 --> 00:13:45.980
But how do the authors know that the landscape
is flatter for MSA?

00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:50.739
Well, they look at the eigenvalues of the
Hessian matrix.

00:13:50.739 --> 00:13:55.519
Short reminder, the Hessian matrix of the
loss function is a square matrix containing

00:13:55.519 --> 00:14:00.360
the second partial derivatives of the loss
function.

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:01.360
In English, please:

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:05.399
We take two directions in our high-dimensional
space that the loss function lives in and

00:14:05.399 --> 00:14:11.399
ask how does the change in one direction change
with respect to the other direction?

00:14:11.399 --> 00:14:16.920
And the eigenvectors of this Hessian matrix
are the principal directions of curvature.

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:20.860
The eigenvalues speak about the magnitude
of that curvature.

00:14:20.860 --> 00:14:27.119
A big Hessian eigenvalue speaks for high curvature,
so for a very curvy and supposedly bumpy loss

00:14:27.119 --> 00:14:28.499
landscape.

00:14:28.499 --> 00:14:32.920
And guess what, MSA suppresses large Hessian
eigenvalues!

00:14:32.920 --> 00:14:38.730
So this makes the loss landscape flatter for
ViT, while the loss landscape of the ResNet

00:14:38.730 --> 00:14:42.660
has high Hessian eigenvalues, thus a higher
curvature.

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:44.910
This is a win for MSA!

00:14:44.910 --> 00:14:50.060
On the not so bright side for MSA, the authors
read another thing from the Hessian of the

00:14:50.060 --> 00:14:51.119
loss function:

00:14:51.119 --> 00:14:57.389
Especially in the regimes of little data,
a lot of Hessian eigenvalues turn out to be

00:14:57.389 --> 00:15:01.300
negative, so the loss landscape is non-convex
for MSA.

00:15:01.300 --> 00:15:06.850
And if you hear “non-convex”, prepare
for local minima that your optimizer might

00:15:06.850 --> 00:15:08.249
not escape from!

00:15:08.249 --> 00:15:14.360
But the good news is that more data also makes
for less negative Hessian eigenvalues, so

00:15:14.360 --> 00:15:16.619
more convex losses.

00:15:16.619 --> 00:15:21.850
Given that in small data regimes the loss
landscape is non-convex, it is again interesting

00:15:21.850 --> 00:15:26.889
to see that ViT does not overfit when training
on small datasets [whisper] local minima!

00:15:26.889 --> 00:15:30.629
So now, what is the difference between MSA
and Convolutions?

00:15:30.629 --> 00:15:36.259
First, something we have discussed already:
convolutions are data-agnostic and MSAs are

00:15:36.259 --> 00:15:37.269
data-specific.

00:15:37.269 --> 00:15:43.110
Secondly, convolutions are channel-specific
where kernels are learned for each channel.

00:15:43.110 --> 00:15:45.569
And MSA does not care on which channel they
are applied.

00:15:45.569 --> 00:15:49.930
In fact, the patches that first consist of
3 channels, for red, green and blue are all

00:15:49.930 --> 00:15:52.540
smashed together into one vector.

00:15:52.540 --> 00:15:56.389
So if, we cite, “Convs are data-agnostic
and channel-specific.

00:15:56.389 --> 00:16:01.630
In contrast, MSAs are data-specific and channel
agnostic.”, then the conclusion of this

00:16:01.630 --> 00:16:05.750
paper is that they should be complementary.

00:16:05.750 --> 00:16:07.709
Nice.

00:16:07.709 --> 00:16:13.720
More interesting for Ms. Coffee Bean is that
“MSAs are low-pass filters, but Convs are

00:16:13.720 --> 00:16:15.489
high-pass filters”.

00:16:15.489 --> 00:16:16.879
What does this mean now?

00:16:16.879 --> 00:16:21.129
A low pass filter lets the low frequencies
pass,

00:16:21.129 --> 00:16:26.989
so things like shape and wide patterns in
the image get through, while sharp details

00:16:26.989 --> 00:16:28.290
do not.

00:16:28.290 --> 00:16:33.470
Think about the low frequencies as the information
that remains after blurring the image.

00:16:33.470 --> 00:16:38.420
Convolutions on the other hand, are high-pass
filters meaning that they let high frequencies

00:16:38.420 --> 00:16:41.680
pass, like very fine edges in texture.

00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:47.800
Therefore, it is no wonder that, we cite “The
results thus suggests that MSAs are shape-biased,

00:16:47.800 --> 00:16:53.319
whereas Convs are texture-biased.“ Which
also confirms findings of previous work.

00:16:53.319 --> 00:17:02.160
The authors see this by doing a Fourier decomposition
of ViT and ResNet features respectively and

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:04.309
comparing the resulting frequencies.

00:17:04.309 --> 00:17:09.780
Also, we are not surprised that CNNs which
are high-pass filters, not filtering out high

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:15.820
frequencies, are vulnerable to high-frequency
noise and adversarial attacks that introduce

00:17:15.820 --> 00:17:21.080
high frequencies into the image – so high
that the human eye cannot even see.

00:17:21.080 --> 00:17:26.450
And the last difference we will discuss here
between MSAs and Convs is that MSAs average

00:17:26.450 --> 00:17:31.700
feature maps and reduce the variance of the
feature maps—an ensembling-like effect.

00:17:31.700 --> 00:17:38.520
The authors support this by showing how MSAs
rather reduce the variance and Convs and MLPs

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:40.930
increase the variance, in their experiments
.

00:17:40.930 --> 00:17:45.980
So it is no wonder that based on this, the
authors move on to the third question of the

00:17:45.980 --> 00:17:50.380
paper of “How can we harmonize MSAs with
Convs?”

00:17:50.380 --> 00:17:57.480
and propose AlterNet which in a nutshell is
a ResNet with MSA blocks after each stage,

00:17:57.480 --> 00:18:01.070
especially at late stages to reduce feature
map variance.

00:18:01.070 --> 00:18:06.380
We will let you decide what you think about
this architecture after you find out about

00:18:06.380 --> 00:18:07.840
all its details.

00:18:07.840 --> 00:18:14.600
Honestly, due to the limited datasets, tasks,
and baselines explored in this paper, we are

00:18:14.600 --> 00:18:17.480
not very impressed by AlterNet.

00:18:17.480 --> 00:18:24.020
But we have seen with ConvNeXt that an enhanced
ResNet can achieve good performance, so we

00:18:24.020 --> 00:18:30.760
do believe the authors’ message here that
marrying Convs with MSA seems like a good

00:18:30.760 --> 00:18:31.760
idea.

00:18:31.760 --> 00:18:36.140
If you want to know all the details that we
missed or got wrong, read the paper.

00:18:36.140 --> 00:18:42.280
We have mixed feelings about its style: On
the one hand, it has a good choice of words

00:18:42.280 --> 00:18:49.640
and flow of ideas, but it is also extremely
dense and sometimes the required details are

00:18:49.640 --> 00:18:51.880
in unexpected places.

00:18:51.880 --> 00:18:56.350
Thanks for watching and sorry you had to wait
for so long for this video to come.

00:18:56.350 --> 00:19:05.160
PhD life is… demanding and Ms. Coffee Bean
isn’t animating herself.

00:19:05.160 --> 00:19:08.780
Okay, bye.

